"isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc1"}, {"id": "f0f27d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "<p>The work functions of Aluminium and Gold are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math> respectively. The ratio of the slope of the stopping potential versus frequency plot for Gold to that of Aluminium is</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "9a43d2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>24</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc4"}, {"id": "bdead1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc5"}, {"id": "54a5ce", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc6"}, {"id": "fdf512", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc7"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc8", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The kinetic energy of emitted electron is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> when the light incident on the metal has wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>. To double the kinetic energy, the incident light must have wavelength:"}, "options": [{"id": "6264c7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc9"}, {"id": "46c46d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dca"}, {"id": "47f967", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dcb"}, {"id": "586bc7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dcc"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dcd", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion $\\mathbf{A}$ and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: Number of photons increases with increase in frequency of light.\nReason R: Maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons increases with the frequency of incident radiation.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:"}, "options": [{"id": "0fd246", "text": "Both $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{R}$ are correct and $\\mathbf{R}$ is the correct explanation of $\\mathbf{A}$.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dce"}, {"id": "7ac33a", "text": "Both $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{R}$ are correct and $\\mathbf{R}$ is NOT the correct explanation of $\\mathbf{A}$.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dcf"}, {"id": "dafb83", "text": "$\\mathbf{A}$ is not correct but $\\mathbf{R}$ is correct.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd0"}, {"id": "8ccbbc", "text": "$\\mathbf{A}$ is correct but $\\mathbf{R}$ is not correct.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelengths of proton and electron having same kinetic energy:<br />(Assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1849</mn></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "261ffb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>43</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd3"}, {"id": "e238c9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>30</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd4"}, {"id": "f7c2f6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>62</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd5"}, {"id": "074bed", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>43</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "A monochromatic light is incident on a metallic plate having work function $\\phi$. An electron, emitted normally to the plate from a point $A$ with maximum kinetic energy, enters a constant magnetic field, perpendicular to the initial velocity of electron. The electron passes through a curve and hits back the plate at a point $B$. The distance between A and B is :<br/>(Given : The magnitude of charge of an electron is e and mass is $\\mathrm{m}, \\mathrm{h}$ is Planck's constant and c is velocity of light. Take the magnetic field exists throughout the path of electron)"}, "options": [{"id": "5a19f1", "text": "$\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd8"}, {"id": "08a777", "text": "$\\sqrt{\\mathrm{m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd9"}, {"id": "d8a164", "text": "$\\sqrt{8 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dda"}, {"id": "a84c0a", "text": "$2 \\sqrt{\\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6ddb"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6ddc", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The de-Broglie wavelength of a particle having kinetic energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>.</mo></math> How much extra energy must be given to this particle so that the de-Broglie wavelength reduces to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;of the initial value?"}, "options": [{"id": "745725", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6ddd"}, {"id": "620b9f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dde"}, {"id": "74abff", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6ddf"}, {"id": "f94940", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "<p>In a photoelectric experiment, ultraviolet light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>280</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math> is used with lithium cathode having work function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>. If the wavelength of incident light is switched to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math>, find out the change in the stopping potential.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>63</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "e74e8b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de2"}, {"id": "70a31f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de3"}, {"id": "0a6574", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de4"}, {"id": "cd9d0e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>&nbsp;particle, a proton and an electron have the same kinetic energy. Which one of the following is correct in case of their de-Broglie wavelength:"}, "options": [{"id": "b5c78d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de7"}, {"id": "3146db", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de8"}, {"id": "e41fa7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6de9"}, {"id": "26213a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dea"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6deb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "The work functions of cesium (Cs) and lithium (Li) metals are 1.9 eV and 2.5 eV , respectively. If we incident a light of wavelength 550 nm on these two metal surfaces, then photo-electric effect is possible for the case of"}, "options": [{"id": "191f23", "text": "Both Cs and Li", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dec"}, {"id": "edb1e6", "text": "Neither Cs nor Li", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6ded"}, {"id": "8426e1", "text": "Cs only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dee"}, {"id": "522c89", "text": "Li only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6def"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The ratio of de-Broglie wavelength of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>-particle and a proton accelerated from rest by the same potential is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math> , the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> is:"}, "options": [{"id": "a68f82", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df1"}, {"id": "e21af3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df2"}, {"id": "f0b7c2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df3"}, {"id": "2dbc7f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "In photoelectric experiment energy of $2.48 \\mathrm{eV}$ irradiates a photo sensitive material. The stopping potential was measured to be $0.5 \\mathrm{~V}$. Work function of the photo sensitive material is :"}, "options": [{"id": "bcbf28", "text": "$1.68 \\mathrm{eV}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df6"}, {"id": "a7954d", "text": "$2.48 \\mathrm{eV}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df7"}, {"id": "01a1b1", "text": "$1.98 \\mathrm{eV}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df8"}, {"id": "1fe763", "text": "$0.5 \\mathrm{eV}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6df9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dfa", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron moving with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> and a photon moving with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math>, have the same D-Broglie wavelength. The ratio of the kinetic energy of the electron to that of a photon is:"}, "options": [{"id": "dbb2c5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dfb"}, {"id": "b9e9f6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dfc"}, {"id": "873614", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dfd"}, {"id": "f19688", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dfe"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dff", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : Emission of electrons in photoelectric effect can be suppressed by applying a sufficiently negative electron potential to the photoemissive substance.\nReason (R) : A negative electric potential, which stops the emission of electrons from the surface of a photoemissive substance, varies linearly with frequency of incident radiation.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "782947", "text": "(A) is false but (R) is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e00"}, {"id": "1e5340", "text": "(A) is true but (R) is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e01"}, {"id": "106ab3", "text": "Both \\((\\mathbf{A})\\) and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) are true and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) is the correct explanation of \\((\\mathbf{A})\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e02"}, {"id": "884f00", "text": "Both \\((\\mathbf{A})\\) and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) are true but \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) is not the correct explanation of \\((\\mathbf{A})\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e03"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e04", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "A moving proton and electron have the same de-Broglie wavelength. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> denote the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>.</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>.</mo></math> and momentum respectively. Then choose the correct option :"}, "options": [{"id": "262253", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e05"}, {"id": "5d4003", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e06"}, {"id": "7b0519", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e07"}, {"id": "fb63ba", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e08"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e09", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "From the photoelectric effect experiment, following observations are made. Identify which of these are correct<br />A. The stopping potential depends only on the work function of the metal.<br />B. The saturation current increases as the intensity of incident light increases.<br />C. The maximum kinetic energy of a photo electron depends on the intensity of the incident light.<br />D. Photoelectric effect can be explained using wave theory of light.<br />Choose the correct answer from the options given below:"}, "options": [{"id": "0d279e", "text": "B, C only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0a"}, {"id": "b193ab", "text": "A, C, D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0b"}, {"id": "67ce33", "text": "B only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0c"}, {"id": "95ddc4", "text": "A, B, D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The de Broglie wavelength of a molecule in a gas at room temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>300</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. If the temperature of the gas is increased to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>600</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>, then the de Broglie wavelength of the same gas molecule becomes"}, "options": [{"id": "50ed5d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0f"}, {"id": "9c071d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e10"}, {"id": "79e48f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e11"}, {"id": "920de2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e12"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e13", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "When radiation of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math> is incident on a metallic surface, the stopping potential of ejected photoelectrons is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. If the same surface is illuminated by radiation of double the previous wavelength, then the stopping potential becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The threshold wavelength of the metal is:"}, "options": [{"id": "11deb4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e14"}, {"id": "37bc90", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e15"}, {"id": "8941e3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e16"}, {"id": "55edb1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e17"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e18", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "An electron and proton are separated by a large distance. The electron starts approaching the proton with energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>eV</mi></math>. The proton captures the electrons and forms a hydrogen atom in second excited state. The resulting photon is incident on a photosensitive metal of threshold wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4000</mn><mi>A</mi></math> What is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectron?"}, "options": [{"id": "69784a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>61</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e19"}, {"id": "3e178c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>41</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e1a"}, {"id": "63aeda", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e1b"}, {"id": "c01fb4", "text": "No photoelectron would be emitted", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e1c"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:10.827Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:10.827Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e22"}], "bonusSections": [], "packDetails": [], "paidFeatures": [], "tags": [], "timeRestrictionSlots": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T11:16:01.504Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-17T04:58:01.343Z", "testType": "PYQs as Chapter Tests", "isResumeBufferAllowed": true, "enableAccessibility": true, "maxAttemptableQuestion": 20, "analysisVideoThumbnail": "", "analysisVideoThumbnailDark": "", "shortTitle": "", "subjectDetails": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "title": "Physics", "shortName": "Phy"}], "testTimerType": "mm", "isMarkForReviewAvailable": true}, "temporaryTestDetail": {"_id": "6a4a1e7a9cbf3c6513046461", "testId": "69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f", "userId": "6914cc9c4d7566258ee3a8fd", "startedAt": "2026-07-05T09:06:02.807Z", "lastAttemptedSection": null, "totalTimeLeft": 40, "bonusAttempt": false, "sections": [{"sectionId": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6e20", "questions": [{"questionId": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6db9", "markedOptions": [], "inputValue": null, "timeTaken": 0, "timeLeftWhenAttempted": null, "status": "notVisited", "_id": "6a4a1e7a9cbf3c6513046463", "uid": "6a4a1e7a9cbf3c6513046464"}, {"questionId": "69de4efac677dc36a88f6dbe", "markedOptions": [], "inputValue": null, "timeTaken": 0, "timeLeftWhenAttempted": null, "status": "notVisited", "_id": "6a4a1e7a9cbf3c6513046465", "uid": "6a4a1e7a9